<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Ezo</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – 1869 CE · capital Hakodate (Goryokaku)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived breakaway regime in Hokkaido set up by shogunate loyalists, whose leaders were chosen by an election of officers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a> in 1868, Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enomoto-takeaki">Enomoto Takeaki</a> sailed north with much of the shogunal navy, picking up defeated soldiers and French military advisers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-brunet">Jules Brunet</a>. In late 1868 his forces landed in Ezo, took the star fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryokaku">Goryokaku</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a> and captured Matsumae.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In January 1869 officers voted to elect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enomoto-takeaki">Enomoto</a> as president, with other posts filled by election, an unprecedented step in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The regime sought recognition from foreign powers as a de facto authority and asked to develop Ezo under a Tokugawa lord, but received only cautious neutrality.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Imperial forces landed in spring 1869. The warship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiyo-maru">Kaiyo Maru</a> had already been lost, and after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijikata-toshizo">Hijikata Toshizo</a> was killed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enomoto-takeaki">Enomoto</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryokaku">Goryokaku</a> in June 1869. He was later pardoned and served the Meiji government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-ezo.jpg" alt="Republic of Ezo" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: DannamEmpire · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Republic_of_Ezo_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1868 CE – 1869 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hakodate (Goryokaku)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Enomoto Takeaki, Matsudaira Taro</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ezo Republic, Ezo-chi government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=republic-of-ezo" aria-label="Republic of Ezo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8106 690 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Ezo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8426 946L8530 1145M8426 946L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8426 946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8426 946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hakodate. <a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=republic-of-ezo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
